# Chapter 7. Comptime: The Heart of Zig

If Zig has a secret superpower, it’s **comptime**. This single feature eliminates the need for a preprocessor, generics, templates, code generation tools, and even most forms of reflection. Comptime allows you to execute arbitrary Zig code during compilation, using the same syntax and semantics as runtime code. No second-class macro language. No template metaprogramming with arcane substitution rules. Just Zig, running at compile time.

This chapter transforms how you think about metaprogramming. Instead of generating code *for* Zig, you write Zig that *generates itself*.

---

## 1. Basic Usage

### The `comptime` Keyword

Any expression can be forced to evaluate at compile time with the `comptime` keyword:

```zig
const std = @import("std");

pub fn main() void {
    const x: comptime_int = 40 + 2;        // Evaluated at compile time
    const y: f64 = comptime 2.5 * 4.0;      // Explicit comptime float
    const z = comptime blk: {
        var sum: u32 = 0;
        var i: u32 = 0;
        while (i < 100) : (i += 1) {
            sum += i;
        }
        break :blk sum;
    };  // z = 4950, computed during compilation

    std.debug.print("z = {}\n", .{z});
}
```

### Comptime Parameters

Functions can accept `comptime` parameters, which must be known at the call site:

```zig
fn makeArray(comptime T: type, comptime len: usize) [len]T {
    var result: [len]T = undefined;
    for (&result, 0..) |*elem, i| {
        elem.* = @as(T, @intCast(i));
    }
    return result;
}

pub fn main() void {
    const ints = makeArray(u32, 5);   // [5]u32 with values 0,1,2,3,4
    const floats = makeArray(f64, 3); // [3]f64 with values 0.0,1.0,2.0

    std.debug.print("{any}\n", .{ints});
}
```

### Compile-Time Type Reflection

The `@typeInfo` builtin returns a compile-time `std.builtin.Type` union describing any type:

```zig
fn printStructFields(comptime T: type) void {
    const info = @typeInfo(T);
    if (info != .struct) {
        @compileError("Expected a struct type, got " ++ @typeName(T));
    }

    comptime var field_count = 0;
    inline for (info.struct.fields) |field| {
        std.debug.print("Field {}: {s} of type {s}\n", .{
            field_count, field.name, @typeName(field.type)
        });
        field_count += 1;
    }
}

const Point = struct { x: f32, y: f32, z: f32 };

pub fn main() void {
    printStructFields(Point);
}
```

### Comptime Conditionals and Loops

Use `comptime if` and `inline for`/`inline while` to generate code conditionally:

```zig
fn genericAdd(comptime T: type, a: T, b: T) T {
    if (comptime T == f32 or T == f64) {
        // Use hardware float addition
        return a + b;
    } else if (comptime T == []const u8) {
        // String concatenation requires allocation (omitted for brevity)
        @compileError("String addition not supported in this example");
    } else {
        return a + b;
    }
}

pub fn main() void {
    const i = genericAdd(i32, 10, 20);      // Uses integer path
    const f = genericAdd(f64, 3.14, 2.86);  // Uses float path
    std.debug.print("{}, {d:.2}\n", .{ i, f });
}
```

