## 4. Competing Approaches

### Versus C: Macros vs. Comptime Functions

```c
// C macro - textual, parentheses required, double-evaluation risk
#define MAX(a, b) ((a) > (b) ? (a) : (b))

// Same macro, but with side effects: MAX(i++, j++) increments twice
```

```zig
// Zig comptime function - type-safe, single evaluation
fn max(comptime T: type, a: T, b: T) T {
    return if (a > b) a else b;
}
```

C macros cannot generate type definitions conditionally without ugly workarounds (`#if` nested inside `#define`). Zig comptime can:

```zig
fn makeStruct(comptime count: usize) type {
    var fields: [count]std.builtin.Type.StructField = undefined;
    inline for (0..count) |i| {
        fields[i] = .{
            .name = comptime std.fmt.comptimePrint("field_{d}", .{i}),
            .type = i32,
            .default_value = null,
            .is_comptime = false,
            .alignment = @alignOf(i32),
        };
    }
    return @Type(.{ .struct = .{
        .layout = .auto,
        .fields = &fields,
        .decls = &.{},
        .is_tuple = false,
    } });
}
```

### Versus Rust: Traits + Macros vs. Comptime Duck Typing

Rust uses traits to constrain generics, requiring explicit trait bounds. Zig uses **duck typing at compile time**: if a type has the required field/method, it works; otherwise, you get a compile error at the usage site.

```rust
// Rust: Must declare trait bound
fn print_debug<T: std::fmt::Debug>(x: T) {
    println!("{:?}", x);
}
```

```zig
// Zig: Any type with a format method works
fn printFormatted(x: anytype) void {
    std.debug.print("{any}\n", .{x});
    // Compile error if x doesn't support 'any' formatting
}
```

This trade-off: Rust catches missing trait bounds earlier (at the function definition) with clearer errors; Zig catches them at call sites, sometimes with more complex error cascades. Zig wins in flexibility (no orphan rules, no coherence issues) but loses in local reasoning.

### Versus Go: `go generate` vs. Comptime

Go's `go generate` is a preprocessor that runs arbitrary commands, generating `.go` files. This is external to the language: tools parse comments like `//go:generate stringer -type=Pill`. Go generics (added in 1.18) solve only a fraction of metaprogramming needs.

Zig’s comptime makes `go generate` obsolete. Everything from enum string conversion to codec generation happens inside the language, without separate tooling or build steps.

### Versus C++: Template Specialization vs. Comptime Switching

```cpp
// C++ - partial specialization for void
template<typename T>
struct Printer { static void print(T x) { std::cout << x; } };

template<>
struct Printer<void> { static void print(void*) { std::cout << "null"; } };
```

```zig
// Zig - comptime switch
fn printValue(x: anytype) void {
    switch (@typeInfo(@TypeOf(x))) {
        .null => std.debug.print("null", .{}),
        else => std.debug.print("{any}", .{x}),
    }
}
```

C++ specializations are separate definitions; Zig uses pattern matching on types. C++ error messages from failed specializations are infamous; Zig’s compiler tells you exactly which `@typeInfo` branch failed.

