## 5. Common Mistakes

### Mistaking Comptime Parameters for Runtime Values

```zig
// WRONG - len is runtime, can't be used in array size
fn badArray(len: usize) [len]u8 {
    var result: [len]u8 = undefined;  // ERROR: len not known at compile time
    return result;
}

// CORRECT - comptime parameter
fn goodArray(comptime len: usize) [len]u8 {
    var result: [len]u8 = undefined;
    return result;
}
```

**The fix**: Add `comptime` to any parameter that must be known at compile time.

### Use-After-Free of Comptime-Allocated Memory

Comptime can use allocators, but memory lifetimes end after compilation:

```zig
const std = @import("std");

pub fn main() void {
    const str = comptime blk: {
        var list = std.ArrayList(u8).init(std.heap.page_allocator);
        defer list.deinit();  // This runs at comptime, not runtime!
        try list.appendSlice("hello");
        break :blk list.items;
    };
    // str is now a slice pointing to memory freed after comptime phase
    std.debug.print("{s}\n", .{str});  // UNDEFINED BEHAVIOR
}
```

**The fix**: Copy comptime-generated data into runtime memory or avoid allocators entirely for comptime data that outlives compilation.

### Overusing `inline for` Causing Code Bloat

```zig
// BLATANT - unrolls all iterations, generating huge code
fn sumArray(comptime T: type, arr: []const T) T {
    var total: T = 0;
    inline for (arr, 0..) |val, i| {
        total += val;  // Unrolled for each i
    }
    return total;
}

// BETTER - use runtime loop when order doesn't matter
fn sumArrayRuntime(comptime T: type, arr: []const T) T {
    var total: T = 0;
    for (arr) |val| {
        total += val;
    }
    return total;
}
```

Use `inline for` only when you need compile-time index information (e.g., generating unique types per iteration).

### `@compileError` in Dead Branches

```zig
fn validate(comptime T: type) void {
    if (comptime T == i32) {
        // OK
    } else {
        @compileError("Only i32 supported");
    }
}

pub fn main() void {
    validate(i32);   // Works
    validate(f32);   // Compile error as expected
}
```

But if you put `@compileError` in a branch that *might* be taken at runtime, you'll get an error regardless:

```zig
// WRONG
fn broken(comptime T: type, val: T) void {
    if (val == 0) {  // val is runtime, condition not comptime-known
        @compileError("Value cannot be zero");
    }
}
```

**The fix**: Ensure conditions guarding `@compileError` are `comptime`-known.

### Forgetting That Comptime Can't Access Runtime State

```zig
fn readFromFile(allocator: std.mem.Allocator) ![]u8 {
    const file = try std.fs.cwd().openFile("data.txt", .{});
    defer file.close();
    const content = try file.readToEndAlloc(allocator, 1024);
    return content;
}

pub fn main() void {
    // WRONG - can't read files at compile time
    const content = comptime readFromFile(std.heap.page_allocator) catch "";
    // ERROR: file system access not allowed in comptime

    // CORRECT - move comptime annotation inside, but still can't read files
}
```

Comptime cannot perform I/O, system calls, or interact with the outside world. It's limited to pure computation and compiler intrinsics.

